I wrote a Jac walker named `EnsureUniqueItem` that on the root node checks if there is exactly one outgoing node of archetype `UniqueItem`. If none exists, it creates one and attaches it. It then reports the attached node. This uses a safe check-then-create pattern with an else clause.

```jac
node UniqueItem {}

walker EnsureUniqueItem {
    has unique_item: UniqueItem | None = None;

    can start with Root entry {
        # Find all outgoing UniqueItem nodes
        unique_items = [here --> UniqueItem];

        if unique_items.count == 1 {
            # Exactly one exists, use it
            self.unique_item = unique_items[0];
        } else if unique_items.count == 0 {
            # None exists, create and attach one
            new_item = UniqueItem();
            here ++> new_item;
            self.unique_item = new_item;
        } else {
            # More than one exists, optionally handle error or pick first
            self.unique_item = unique_items[0];
        }

        report self.unique_item;
    }
}
```
This meets the requirement: from the root node, it ensures exactly one `UniqueItem` node attached via outgoing edge, creating it if missing, and reports that node.

I created a Jac program that includes:

- A `Person` node archetype with `name: str` and `age: int` fields.
- A `Root` node archetype as the graph root.
- A walker `FindOlderPersons` that starts at `Root`, traverses outgoing edges, filters for `Person` nodes with `age > 30`, collects them in a typed report list.
- In the `entry` block, it creates a sample graph with three people of varying ages connected from a `Root` node, runs the walker, and prints the names of the reported persons.

```jac
node Person {
    has name: str;
    has age: int;
}

node Root {}

walker FindOlderPersons {
    has reports: list[list[Person]];

    can find with Root entry {
        # Collect all Person nodes reachable from Root with age > 30
        persons: list[Person] = [];
        for p in [-->][?:Person] {
            if p.age > 30 {
                persons += [p];
            }
        }
        report persons;
    }
}

with entry {
    # Create root node
    root = Root();

    # Create Person nodes
    alice = Person(name="Alice", age=25);
    bob = Person(name="Bob", age=35);
    carol = Person(name="Carol", age=40);

    # Connect root to persons
    root ++> alice;
    root ++> bob;
    root ++> carol;

    # Spawn walker from root
    result = root spawn FindOlderPersons();

    # Extract reported list of persons
    older_persons: list[Person] = result.reports[0];

    # Print names of persons over 30
    for person in older_persons {
        print(person.name);
    }
}
```
This fulfills the request with a typed report list and prints the names of persons older than 30.

