I created a walker named `EnsureUniqueItem` with an ability triggered on the root node type `Root`. It checks if there is exactly one outgoing node of archetype `UniqueItem`. If none exist, it creates one and attaches it from the root. It then reports the attached node. This uses the safe check-then-create pattern.

```jac
node UniqueItem {}

walker EnsureUniqueItem {
    can start with Root entry {
        # Find all outgoing UniqueItem nodes from root
        unique_items = [here --> UniqueItem];

        if unique_items.count == 0 {
            # Create and attach a new UniqueItem node
            new_item = UniqueItem();
            here ++> new_item;
            report new_item;
        } elif unique_items.count == 1 {
            # Exactly one exists, report it
            report unique_items[0];
        } else {
            # More than one exists, report all or handle as needed
            # Here, we just report the first one for simplicity
            report unique_items[0];
        }
    }
}
```

I created a Jac walker named `SquareStats` that takes a list of numbers as input. It computes the squares of the numbers, reports the list of squares first, then calculates the sum and average of the squares and reports that dictionary second.

```jac
walker SquareStats {
    has numbers: list = [];
    has squares: list = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        # Compute squares
        self.squares = [x * x for x in self.numbers];
        # Report list of squares
        report self.squares;

        # Compute sum and average
        let total = 0;
        for val in self.squares {
            total += val;
        }
        let avg = 0;
        if len(self.squares) > 0 {
            avg = total / len(self.squares);
        }

        # Report dictionary with sum and average
        report {
            "sum": total,
            "average": avg
        };
    }
}
```

