I wrote a Jac walker named `UniqueItemEnsurer` that starts at the root node, checks for exactly one outgoing node of archetype `UniqueItem`, creates one if missing, and reports the attached node. It uses the safe check-then-create pattern with an else clause on the visit to detect missing nodes.

```jac
node UniqueItem {
    # Define any attributes if needed
}

walker UniqueItemEnsurer {
    has unique_item: UniqueItem | None = None;

    can start with Root entry {
        # Visit outgoing UniqueItem nodes from root
        visit [-->][?:UniqueItem] else {
            # If no UniqueItem node found, create one and attach it
            new_item = UniqueItem();
            here ++> new_item;
            self.unique_item = new_item;
        }
    }

    can start with UniqueItem entry {
        # If found, store the unique item node
        self.unique_item = here;
    }

    can finish with Root exit {
        # Report the unique item node attached to root
        report self.unique_item;
    }
}
```

This walker:
- On starting at the root, visits outgoing `UniqueItem` nodes.
- If none found, creates one and attaches it.
- Stores the found or created node in `self.unique_item`.
- Reports the stored node on finishing the root visit.

