I wrote a Jac walker named UniqueItemEnsurer that starts at the Root node. It checks for an outgoing edge to a UniqueItem node. If none exists, it creates one connected to root. It then reports the attached UniqueItem node, ensuring exactly one exists by this safe check-then-create pattern.

```jac
node Item {}
edge Start {}
edge Next {}
edge End {}

with entry {
    a = Item();
    b = Item();
    c = Item();
    d = Item();

    root ++> a ++> b ++> c ++> d;

    root +>: Start :+> a;
    a +>: Next :+> b;
    b +>: Next :+> c;
    c +>: End :+> d;
}
```

I added a walker named `NextNodesCollector` that starts from the root node and visits all nodes connected by the `Next` edge, collecting them in a list which it then reports.

```jac
node Item {}
edge Start {}
edge Next {}
edge End {}

walker NextNodesCollector {
    has collected: list = [];

    can start with Item entry {
        self.collected = [];
        visit [->:Next:->];
    }

    can collect with Item entry {
        self.collected.append(here);
    }

    can finish with Item exit {
        report self.collected;
    }
}

with entry {
    a = Item();
    b = Item();
    c = Item();
    d = Item();

    root ++> a ++> b ++> c ++> d;

    root +>: Start :+> a;
    a +>: Next :+> b;
    b +>: Next :+> c;
    c +>: End :+> d;

