The Jac walker "EnsureUniqueItem" is created to operate at the Root node entry. It checks for outgoing nodes of archetype "UniqueItem". If none exist, it creates one and connects it to the root. It then reports the attached UniqueItem node. This uses a safe check-then-create pattern to ensure exactly one such node exists.

```jac
node UniqueItem {}

walker EnsureUniqueItem {
    can start with Root entry {
        # Find all UniqueItem nodes connected from root
        unique_items = [here -->][?:UniqueItem];

        # If none found, create one and connect it
        if unique_items.count() == 0 {
            new_item = UniqueItem();
            here ++> new_item;
            unique_items = [new_item];
        }

        # Report the single UniqueItem node (first in list)
        report unique_items[0];
    }
}
```

I wrote a Jac walker named `SquareStats` that takes a list of numbers as input. It computes the list of their squares and reports it as the first report. Then it calculates the sum and average of the squares and reports a dictionary containing these values as the second report.

```jac
walker SquareStats {
    can start with list entry {
        # Compute squares of the numbers
        squares = [];
        for num in here {
            squares.append(num * num);
        }
        report squares;

        # Compute sum and average of squares
        total = 0;
        for sq in squares {
            total += sq;
        }
        avg = 0;
        if squares.len() > 0 {
            avg = total / squares.len();
        }
        stats = {"sum": total, "average": avg};
        report stats;
    }
}
