The original code had syntax errors due to missing semicolons and incorrect chaining of typed edge connections. In Jac, connection chains with generic edges (`++>`) can be chained, but typed edge connections (`+>: EdgeType :+>`) must be separate statements ending with semicolons. The corrected code ends the first generic connection chain with a semicolon and splits the typed connections into individual statements also ending with semicolons.

```jac
node Item {}
edge Start {}
edge Next {}
edge End {}

with entry {
    a = Item();
    b = Item();
    c = Item();
    d = Item();

    root ++> a ++> b ++> c ++> d;

    root +>: Start :+> a;
    a +>: Next :+> b;
    b +>: Next :+> c;
    c +>: End :+> d;
}
```

```jac
node Item {}
edge Start {}
edge Next {}
edge End {}

walker NextCollector {
    has collected: list = [];

    can start with Root entry {
        self.collected = [];
        self.collect_next(root);
        report self.collected;
    }

    def collect_next(node: node) {
        self.collected.append(node);
        for nxt in [node ->:Next->] {
            self.collect_next(nxt);
        }
    }
}

with entry {
    a = Item();
    b = Item();
    c = Item();
    d = Item();

    root ++> a ++> b ++> c ++> d;

    root +>: Start :+> a;
    a +>: Next :+> b;
    b +>: Next :+> c;
    c +>: End :+> d;
}
```
I added a walker named `NextCollector` that recursively collects all nodes connected by `Next` edges starting from `root`. The `start` ability initializes collection and reports the list. The helper method `collect_next` appends the current node and recursively visits all nodes connected by `Next` edges.

